\subsection{Deciding Halting for a Finite Deterministic Machine}

An undecidable question can have a decidable fragment if the
description restricts what counts as a configuration. Assume a fixed
input has already been loaded into a machine with a finite set of
complete configurations. Each nonterminal configuration has exactly
one successor and each terminal one has none. A configuration
includes all data relevant to the next step, including control
position, bounded storage, and any input cursor. The procedure
returns an exact step count for a halt or the start and length
of a repeating cycle. No timeout is interpreted as nontermination.

\begin{sudocode}[Classify the orbit from one configuration]
\Function{Orbit}{$c,\mathrm{next},\mathrm{terminal}$}
  \State $P\gets$ empty map; $t\gets0$
  \Loop
    \If{$\mathrm{terminal}(c)$}
      \State \Return halt at $t$
    \EndIf
    \If{$c\in\operatorname{dom}(P)$}
      \State \Return cycle $(P[c],t-P[c])$
    \EndIf
    \State $P[c]\gets t$ \Comment{Record first visit only}
    \State $c\gets\mathrm{next}(c)$ \Comment{One complete step}
    \State $t\gets t+1$
  \EndLoop
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
Consider a two-bit register $r$, initially $0$. Halt at $2$;
otherwise set $r$ to $(r+1)\bmod 4$. The first two visits
store $P[0]=0$ and $P[1]=1$. At step $2$ the terminal test
succeeds, so the classifier returns halt at $2$. Now change the
update to $(r+2)\bmod4$, taking initial value $1$ instead.
The visits are $1$ at step $0$, $3$ at step $1$, and $1$
at step $2$. The cycle result is $(0,2)$. Even though the
terminal state still exists in the machine, it is not on this
initial orbit.

\Cref{fig:visual-12} shows why the existence of a terminal configuration
is not enough to classify an initial orbit.

