\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
At loop entry $c$ is exactly the configuration after $t$ steps
and $P$ contains the first visit of every earlier nonterminal
configuration. After a repeat, determinism forces the same
successor sequence forever, proving divergence for this input.
If the space has $N$ configurations, at most $N$ entries are
stored; a repeat or halt is found after at most $N$ transitions.
Equality of instruction addresses alone would not justify
this conclusion. The finite bound, which may itself be
exponential in storage width, is the crucial restriction;
the argument does not decide halting for unbounded machines.

\section{Exercises}\label{sec:comp-exercises}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item Prove that the language $L = \{\langle M \rangle \mid L(M) = \emptyset\}$ is undecidable (by Rice's theorem or direct reduction).
  
  \item Show that the halting problem is semi-decidable.
  
  \item Prove that $\mathbf{P} \subseteq \mathbf{NP}$.
  
  \item Show that 3-SAT is in $\mathbf{NP}$.
  
  \item Prove that if $\mathbf{P} = \mathbf{NP}$, then every problem in $\mathbf{NP}$ can be solved in polynomial time.
  
  \item Show that the problem ``does this program always return a positive value?'' is undecidable.
  
  \item Design a sound but incomplete analysis for detecting potential null pointer dereferences.
  
  \item Prove that type checking for the simply-typed lambda calculus is decidable in linear time.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Chapter Summary}\label{sec:comp-summary}

This chapter has established the fundamental limits of computation and program analysis:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Turing machines define the class of computable functions (Church--Turing thesis).
  \item The halting problem is undecidable: no algorithm can determine whether an arbitrary program terminates.
  \item Rice's theorem generalizes this: no non-trivial semantic property of programs is decidable.
  \item Complexity classes (P, NP, EXPTIME) classify problems by their computational difficulty.
  \item Most program analysis problems are undecidable, but tractable approximations exist.
  \item Abstract interpretation provides a systematic framework for sound approximation.
\end{itemize}

These limitations are not cause for despair. They define the boundaries of what is possible, and within those boundaries, there is enormous room for innovation. The rest of this book explores what can be done within those boundaries: how to design type systems that catch errors, how to build analyses that find bugs, how to construct optimizers that improve performance, and how to verify that compilers preserve semantics.

\begin{historical}
The theory of computability was born in the 1930s from the work of Church, Turing, G\"odel, and Kleene, motivated by foundational questions in mathematics (Hilbert's Entscheidungsproblem). Complexity theory emerged in the 1960s and 1970s with the work of Hartmanis, Stearns, Cook, and Karp. The application of these results to programming language theory---particularly the realization that program analysis is fundamentally limited by Rice's theorem---was a key insight of the 1970s that motivated the development of abstract interpretation and dataflow analysis.
\end{historical}
